skill: writing-style
tools: claude,kiro,codex
--- playback ---
task: Answer the following in 4–8 sentences. This is a dry-run description task, not a live teach: do NOT call any file-editing tool and do NOT modify any file — describe only. (a) I say `new rule: prefer "to" over "in order to"` — show the exact `## Learned rules` entry you would append and confirm what you'd do. (b) Separately, I say `I prefer shorter sentences` — what do you do with that, and why?
expect: A correct answer distinguishes the explicit-teach trigger from a conversational non-trigger, per the skill's Learning loop section. (1) For `new rule: …` — recognises it as one of the strict prefix triggers (`new rule:` / `add rule:` / `style rule:`) and shows an entry it would APPEND to the `## Learned rules` section, newest at top, in the skill's shape: a date-stamped line carrying the rule text, e.g. "- <date> · prefer \"to\" over \"in order to\"" optionally with `· *example:*` and `· source: explicit teach` fields. It should note the append lands under `## Learned rules` (not silently edited into a body section — promotion into Voice/Vocabulary/etc. is a separate, later step) and that it confirms with a short line afterward. (2) For "I prefer shorter sentences" — recognises this is NOT a trigger (it lacks the `new rule:`/`add rule:`/`style rule:` prefix), so it does NOT capture a rule; it treats the remark conversationally to avoid false-positive captures. Wrong answers to flag: treating "I prefer…" or "stop using…" as a capture trigger; writing the new rule directly into a body section instead of appending to `## Learned rules`; omitting the date stamp or the newest-at-top ordering; claiming it would edit a file when the prompt said not to.
--- enact ---
task: Format this passage in my house style, then show your work. This is a scoped, one-off formatting task, not a session start: do not open with an offer to promote pending learned rules — go straight to the task (still apply the style guide, learned rules included). The passage: "We should arguably leverage the new pipeline to utilize our existing infrastructure. This is a HUGE win for the team! We will circle back on the remaining items due to the fact that they are somewhat lower priority." Output the rewritten passage first, with no preamble before it, then a bulleted change log citing the section each edit came from.
expect: A correct answer does two things the skill's Formatter section requires. (1) It outputs a REWRITTEN passage that actually corrects the seeded violations per the guide — the business-speak "leverage" and "circle back" are gone (e.g. "use", "return to"/"follow up on"); the long words are replaced ("utilize" → "use", "due to the fact that" → "because"); the hedges "arguably" and "somewhat" are cut or made definite; the exclamation mark is removed; and "HUGE" in all-caps is de-capitalised (emphasis via bold/italics or plain words, never UPPERCASE). The rewrite reads as plain, active, first-person prose. (2) It then emits a bulleted CHANGE LOG — one bullet per substantive edit, each citing the guide section the rule came from (Vocabulary, Voice, Punctuation, Emphasis, etc.), e.g. "Vocabulary: 'leverage' → 'use'" or "Emphasis: removed UPPERCASE 'HUGE'". Wrong answers to flag: echoing the passage unchanged or nearly so; leaving any of leverage/utilize/circle back/due to the fact that/the exclamation mark/the all-caps word in place; producing a rewrite with NO change log, or a change log that doesn't cite sections; adding a chatty preamble before the rewritten passage instead of leading with the text.
